{"classified_as":[{"_label":"Collection Item","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404024","type":"Type"}],"id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/actor/1579","identified_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"preferred term","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670"}],"content":"Boris Mirski Gallery","type":"Name"},{"classified_as":[{"_label":"former name","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435719"}],"content":"Boris Mirski Art Gallery","type":"Name"},{"classified_as":[{"_label":"first_name","id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/terms/local/first_name"}],"content":"","type":"Name"},{"classified_as":[{"_label":"last_name","id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/terms/local/last_name"}],"content":"Boris Mirski Gallery","type":"Name"}],"produced_by":{"carried_out_by":[],"timespan":{"type":"TimeSpan"},"type":"Production"},"referred_to_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"biography statement","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435422"}],"content":"The Boris Mirski Gallery (1944–1979) was a Boston art gallery owned by Boris Chaim Mirski (1898–1974). The gallery was known for exhibiting key figures in Boston Expressionism, New York and international modern art styles and non-western art. For years, the gallery dominated with both figurative and African work.  As an art dealer, Mirski was known for supporting young, emerging artists, including many Jewish-Americans, as well as artists of color, women artists and immigrants. As a result of Mirski's avant-garde approach to art and diversified approach to dealing art, the gallery was at the center of Boston's burgeoning modern mid-century art scene, as well as instrumental in the birth and development of Boston Expressionism, the most significant branch of American Figurative Expressionism. (Source: Wikipedia, 2025)","type":"LinguisticObject"}],"representation":[],"type":"Actor"}